{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Convert the staged ISO-8859-1 filenames into valid UTF-8 filenames under converted/, retaining directory structure and file contents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The two non-UTF-8 source names were converted to UTF-8 byte sequences as converted/café.txt and converted/résumé.txt. Both destinations were directly shown to be valid UTF-8 and to have SHA-256 hashes matching their corresponding sources. The verification accounted for every staged source; the prepared tree contained no nested source directories requiring additional structure replication.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-ab15ae59240844aa","cmd-59e981bebe1a454c","cmd-21b2fd4ff6fc4cb9","cmd-cb1e3977f1bd4434"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Preserve the imported tree.","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three original imported filename byte sequences remained present. Their final content hashes match the preparation baseline, including the two Latin-1 names and imported/plain.txt; they were still present after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-ab15ae59240844aa","cmd-21b2fd4ff6fc4cb9","cmd-cb1e3977f1bd4434"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Report any existing UTF-8 destination collision without overwriting the destination or silently dropping the source.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The pre-existing converted/plain.txt retained its baseline SHA-256 hash, imported/plain.txt remained present with its distinct original hash, and migration-collision-report.txt explicitly recorded that the existing destination and source were preserved. This state was revalidated after restart.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-075a6d378ad94b47","cmd-59e981bebe1a454c","cmd-21b2fd4ff6fc4cb9","cmd-cb1e3977f1bd4434"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 remained on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8.x86_64 before and after execution. The migration used the platform-installed Python 3, no package changes were observed, and the final package-health check passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45ab8ee54b734735","cmd-cb1e3977f1bd4434","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":1,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. cmd-59e981bebe1a454c created the required converted files and collision report, directly serving the task outcomes. cmd-e775df73c49542cd scheduled the provider-required restart; its transient unit disappeared through that restart, whose completion and recovery were directly verified. Other terminal commands were read-only discovery or validation. Final lifecycle evidence showed no failed services, no package damage, and no new network exposure; PID and systemd-private-directory changes were normal consequences of the restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-59e981bebe1a454c","cmd-e775df73c49542cd","cmd-cb1e3977f1bd4434","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct baseline-to-final evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, the restart was acknowledged, connectivity was disrupted during reboot, a different boot identity appeared, and system, platform, migration data, collision handling, and relevant services recovered. The fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the boot transition.","limitations":[]}
